---
# Slice 1's probe (netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2) asks "is a workload with no in-cluster caller
# unreachable" — that's the whole question for the six apps in that slice. Slice 2's workloads
# (sonarr, radarr, prowlarr, qbittorrent) DO have allowed callers, so the question is different:
# does each policy admit exactly its listed callers and nobody else. This probe asserts that
# from a pod that is on nobody's allow list — it carries no `app` label any of the four slice-2
# policies admit, cross-checked against their matchExpressions/matchLabels at write time.
#
# THE POSITIVE DIRECTION IS DELIBERATELY NOT PROBED HERE. It is already proven by two things that
# run on every deploy anyway: roles/k8s/janitorr/tasks/verify.yml opens TCP to sonarr, radarr and
# jellyfin from inside the janitorr pod, and roles/k8s/sonarr/tasks/verify.yml calls sonarr's API
# from the host. Both fail their deploy if the fencing is wrong. Do not add a redundant positive
# probe here — extend one of those two instead.
#
# This pod carries NO netpol-baseline label, so the baseline does not select it either. That is
# also the point — it stands in for a compromised workload trying to move laterally.
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: netpol-baseline-probe-slice2
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  # Deliberately BELOW the `kubectl wait --timeout=180s` in tasks/main.yml, not equal to it. The
  # two clocks start at different moments, so equal values make it a coin flip which one fires on
  # a slow cold image pull — and the two failures read completely differently. Lower here means
  # the Job is always the clock that expires first, so the pod is terminal (and its logs final)
  # by the time the wait gives up and the log fetch runs. Costs no wall time either way: with
  # backoffLimit: 0 a failed Job never satisfies --for=condition=complete, so the wait burns its
  # full 180s regardless.
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 120
  backoffLimit: 0
  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 86400
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: netpol-baseline-probe-slice2
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      enableServiceLinks: false
      automountServiceAccountToken: false
      containers:
        - name: probe
          image: {{ netpol_baseline_probe_image }}
          command:
            - sh
            - -c
            - |
              if ! nc -w 5 -z traefik 80; then
                echo "CONTROL FAILED: cannot reach traefik:80, so nothing below is attributable"
                echo "to the policy. Check DNS and the traefik Service."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "control ok: traefik:80 reachable"

              if ! nc -w 5 -z homepage 3000; then
                echo "NEGATIVE CONTROL FAILED: cannot reach the unfenced homepage:3000 either."
                echo "This pod cannot open connections at all, so the assertions below would pass"
                echo "whether or not the policies work."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "negative control ok: unfenced homepage:3000 reachable"

              # This pod carries no app label that sonarr's policy admits. Its allowed callers are
              # traefik, janitorr, configarr, monitor-bridge, autofix-bridge, bazarr and prowlarr
              # (roles/k8s/sonarr/templates/networkpolicy.yaml.j2). Port hardcoded from
              # ansible/inventory/host_vars/daniel-box.yml's sonarr containers_list entry — a
              # wrong value here fails loudly (this probe) rather than silently.
              if nc -w 5 -z sonarr 8989; then
                echo "POLICY NOT ENFORCED: reached sonarr directly from a pod that is not one of"
                echo "its allowed callers. Either netpol_baseline_enforced is false, or the label"
                echo "is missing from sonarr's POD TEMPLATE, or sonarr's policy admits too much."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "isolated: sonarr unreachable from an unlisted caller"

              # qbittorrent's policy admits traefik, sonarr, radarr and its own reach-probe
              # (roles/k8s/qbittorrent/templates/networkpolicy.yaml.j2). This pod is none of those.
              # Port hardcoded rather than referencing qbittorrent_k8s_webui_port
              # (roles/k8s/qbittorrent/defaults/main.yml): this role's tasks reach it via
              # include_role (ansible/deploy.yml), which does not expose another role's
              # defaults/main.yml unless that include sets `public: true` — none in this repo do.
              if nc -w 5 -z qbittorrent 8080; then
                echo "POLICY NOT ENFORCED: reached qbittorrent from a pod that is not sonarr or"
                echo "radarr."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "isolated: qbittorrent unreachable from an unlisted caller"
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 10m
              memory: 16Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 200m
              memory: 64Mi
